                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the applicant pool and the mandate`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `38 qualified applications, 10 seats, and a renewed mandate that now <strong>measures every choice</strong>. Deep-tech offers the highest exit value but the lowest two-year survival (~30%); SaaS survives best (~58%) but raises smaller rounds; marketplaces carry network-effect risk; social ventures are mission-aligned but harder to fund. Diagnose the portfolio logic: which venture types drive <strong>survival</strong>, which drive <strong>follow-on funding</strong>, and exactly where the two KPIs <em>pull against each other</em>. Get this wrong and you will optimize a single metric and fail the other.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Select the cohort from 38 applicants`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Choose the 10 ventures. The cohort's <strong>blended</strong> survival and funding — not any single winner — decides whether you clear 60% and R$25 milhões. How many high-variance deep-tech <em>swings</em> can you carry against reliable SaaS <em>base hits</em>, and do you reserve a seat for a mission-aligned social venture? <strong>Diversify deliberately</strong> so one sector's failure cannot sink the cohort. Stack it with moonshots and your blended survival craters; pick only safe SaaS and your follow-on funding falls short.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Allocate scarce, rival support`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `You can fund ~2.400 mentor-hours and R$1,8 milhões in micro-grants — <em>not enough to fully serve 10 ventures</em>. Choose the allocation logic: <strong>equal</strong> (fair, weak outcomes), <strong>tiered</strong> (more to the strongest), or <strong>milestone-gated</strong> (released as ventures clear checkpoints). Then decide whether to fund the <strong>R$700 mil fundraising-readiness program</strong> — and from where. Spread support thinly and you tax the winners to subsidize the doomed; cut readiness and your follow-on funding lands short of the mandate.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Set graduation criteria & defend the cohort`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Set the <strong>graduation / stage-gate criteria</strong> — revenue traction, validated unit economics, a committed lead investor, mentor sign-off — calibrated to protect the survival statistic <em>without graduating zombies</em> or culling the cohort to optics-damaging size. Then run the mid-cohort review: two ventures have missed every milestone — double down, redirect, or off-ramp them. Finally defend your projected survival, follow-on funding and resource efficiency to the agency board against the mandate. Loose gates inflate survival with zombies and cost you your credibility.`,

                    r1_driver_label:        `Which venture type does your portfolio thesis lean on to clear BOTH KPIs at once?`,
                    r1_tension_label:       `Where do the survival and follow-on funding KPIs pull in opposite directions?`,
                    r1_signal_label:        `Which observable applicant signal do you trust most to predict the mandated outcomes?`,
                    r1_rigor_label:         `How rigorously does your team operationalize the dual mandate before selecting? (%)`,
                    r1_rigor_readout:       `Portfolio thesis rigor:`,
                    driver_both:            `Neither alone — survival is driven by reliable types, funding by high-value types; the cohort must blend both deliberately`,
                    driver_deeptech:        `Deep-tech — its exit value will carry the funding KPI and pull survival up with it`,
                    driver_saas:            `SaaS — its high survival will satisfy the mandate and the funding will follow`,
                    driver_social:          `Social ventures — mission alignment will impress the agency on both counts`,
                    tension_variance:       `High-funding deep-tech has the lowest survival — chasing funding lowers survival, and chasing survival starves funding`,
                    tension_none:           `They don't really conflict — strong ventures score well on both`,
                    tension_cost:           `The only tension is budget — both KPIs rise if we simply spend more`,
                    tension_timing:         `It is purely a timing issue that resolves itself by year two`,
                    signal_track:           `Founder track record + capital efficiency + traction — signals that predict survival and fundability`,
                    signal_hype:            `Pitch polish and headline-grabbing market size`,
                    signal_coach:           `Coachability alone — we can fix everything else in the program`,
                    signal_random:          `No signal predicts well; select broadly and hope`,

                    r2_alloc_label:         `Fill the 10 cohort seats across venture types (must total 10)`,
                    r2_seats_readout:       `Seats filled:`,
                    r2_seats_cap:           `/ 10`,
                    r2_diversify_label:     `How hard do you diversify so one sector's failure cannot sink the whole cohort?`,
                    seat_saas:              `SaaS base hits — ~58% survival, smaller rounds`,
                    seat_deeptech:          `Deep-tech swings — ~30% survival, highest exit value`,
                    seat_marketplace:       `Marketplaces — network-effect risk, moderate rounds`,
                    seat_social:            `Social ventures — mission-aligned, harder to fund`,
                    div_balanced:           `Deliberately balanced — no single type dominates, variance is managed`,
                    div_concentrate:        `Concentrate on one type to maximize a single metric`,
                    div_ignore:             `Ignore diversification — pick the highest projected scores regardless of mix`,

                    r3_logic_label:         `How do you allocate the 2.400 mentor-hours and R$1,8M micro-grants across the 10 ventures?`,
                    r3_readiness_label:     `Do you fund the R$700 mil fundraising-readiness program (demo days, investor matching, legal clinics)?`,
                    r3_concentration_label: `How much of the micro-grant pool do you concentrate on the most fundable ventures rather than spreading it? (%)`,
                    r3_concentration_readout: `Grant concentration on top ventures:`,
                    r3_mentor_label:        `Mentor-hours steered to the two most fundable ventures to close their rounds (of 2.400)`,
                    r3_mentor_hint:         `A concentrated push of ~600–900 hours closes the two most fundable rounds; the rest is shared across the cohort.`,
                    logic_milestone:        `Milestone-gated — support released as ventures clear checkpoints, concentrating on those earning it`,
                    logic_tiered:           `Tiered — more to the most fundable and survivable from the start`,
                    logic_equal:            `Equal — 240 hours and R$180K each, the same for every venture`,
                    ready_sponsor:          `Yes — fund it and raise the R$700K from sponsors so the grant pool stays intact`,
                    ready_grants:           `Yes — fund it out of the micro-grant pool`,
                    ready_cut:              `No — cut it to protect the micro-grants`,

                    r4_gate_label:          `Set the graduation / stage-gate criteria`,
                    r4_offramp_label:       `Mid-cohort review: two ventures have missed every milestone. What do you do?`,
                    r4_board_label:         `The agency board presses: is your 60% survival real or inflated by zombies?`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you defend portfolio discipline over chasing a single headline number? (%)`,
                    gate_calibrated:        `Calibrated — revenue traction, validated unit economics, committed lead investor, mentor sign-off`,
                    gate_loose:             `Loose — graduate everyone who is still incorporated, to keep the headcount high`,
                    gate_brutal:            `Brutal — cull anything short of a closed Series A, even promising ventures`,
                    off_offramp:            `Off-ramp both and redirect their support to a strong venture about to close its round`,
                    off_redirect:           `Redirect half the support, keep them on probation`,
                    off_loyalty:            `Keep pouring support into both out of loyalty — they might still turn around`,
                    board_evidence:         `Real — here is the gated evidence: revenue, unit economics and committed investors per graduate`,
                    board_optics:           `We graduated the full cohort — the headcount looks strong`,
                    board_defer:            `We will know in two years; trust the process`,

                    warn_rigor_range:       `Portfolio thesis rigor must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_seats_negative:    `Seat counts cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_seats_total:       `You must fill exactly 10 seats — there are only 10. Re-balance the cohort.`,
                    warn_concentration_range: `Grant concentration must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_mentor_range:      `Mentor-hours must be between 0 and 2.400.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_driver_right:  `<strong>That's the portfolio insight.</strong> No single type clears both KPIs — reliable SaaS carries survival, high-value deep-tech carries funding, and only a deliberate blend hits 60% AND R$25 milhões. You are managing a portfolio, not picking one winner.`,
                    coach_r1_deeptech:      `<strong>You're optimizing for headlines, not refunding.</strong> Deep-tech has huge exit value but ~30% two-year survival. Lean the thesis on it and your blended survival craters below the 60% mandate. Upside is not the same as the survival the agency measures.`,
                    coach_r1_saas:          `<strong>Safe, but watch the other KPI.</strong> SaaS survives best (~58%), so it protects survival — but its seed rounds here are small and your follow-on funding will fall short of R$25 milhões. You can't satisfy the mandate on survival alone.`,
                    coach_r1_social:        `<strong>Mission alignment doesn't pay the mandate.</strong> Social ventures are valuable and survive reasonably, but they raise the least follow-on funding. Leaning the whole thesis on them misses the R$25 milhões target badly.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the dual-KPI logic AND located the real tension: the highest-funding deep-tech has the lowest survival, so the two mandates pull against each other. That is exactly the lens that lets you build a cohort that clears both.`,
                    coach_r1_cost:          `<strong>It isn't just a budget problem.</strong> Spending more doesn't change that deep-tech survives at ~30%. The tension is structural — variance versus survival — not a line you can buy your way out of with the fixed R$6,4 milhões.`,
                    coach_r1_notension:     `<strong>That's how you fail one KPI.</strong> The two mandates genuinely conflict: the ventures that raise the most are the least likely to survive. Pretend there's no tension and you'll optimize one metric and miss the other.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>Selecting without operationalizing the mandate is guessing.</strong> If you don't translate '60% survival and R$25 milhões' into a target cohort mix, you'll pick on instinct and discover the gap only when the agency audits. Do the portfolio math first.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A managed portfolio.</strong> No single type dominates, so one sector's failure won't sink the cohort, and the blend gives you a real shot at both KPIs. That is variance management, not luck.`,
                    coach_r2_saidbalanced:  `<strong>You said balanced but loaded one type heavily.</strong> A cohort with six-plus of a single venture type isn't diversified — its fate rides on one sector. Spread the seats so no one failure is fatal.`,
                    coach_r2_concentrate:   `<strong>Concentration maximizes one metric and exposes you.</strong> Loading a single type chases one KPI while leaving the cohort hostage to that sector. If it underperforms, both KPIs go with it. Manage the variance.`,
                    coach_r2_ignore:        `<strong>Picking top scores regardless of mix is not portfolio thinking.</strong> Without deliberate diversification a single sector's downturn takes the whole cohort with it. The blended outcome is what the mandate measures.`,
                    coach_r2_variance:      `<strong>Variance overload.</strong> Six or more deep-tech swings looks ambitious, but at ~30% survival your blended rate drops near 38% — well below the 60% mandate. You're optimizing for exit headlines and about to lose R$3,5 milhões in refunding.`,
                    coach_r2_safe:          `<strong>Single-KPI optimization.</strong> An almost all-SaaS cohort gets you near 58% survival, but SaaS seed rounds are small — your follow-on funding lands around R$14 milhões against the R$25 milhões requirement. You protected one KPI and failed the other.`,
                    coach_r3_milestone:     `<strong>Milestone-gated allocation is the discipline the mandate rewards.</strong> Releasing support as ventures clear checkpoints concentrates scarce mentor-hours and grants on the ventures earning them — the strongest lift to both survival and funding per real consumed.`,
                    coach_r3_tiered:        `<strong>Tiered allocation beats equal.</strong> Putting more behind the most fundable and survivable from the start lifts expected outcomes — not as adaptive as milestone gates, but far better than spreading thinly.`,
                    coach_r3_equal:         `<strong>The equal-allocation reflex.</strong> 240 hours and R$180K each feels fair, but your two most fundable ventures needed a concentrated push to close their rounds and your two weakest will fail regardless. You've taxed the winners to subsidize the doomed.`,
                    coach_r3_sponsor:       `<strong>Smart financing.</strong> Funding the readiness program from sponsor money keeps the micro-grant pool intact AND invests in fundability — demo days, investor matching and legal clinics are exactly what lifts follow-on funding toward R$25 milhões.`,
                    coach_r3_grants:        `<strong>Right program, costly source.</strong> The readiness program genuinely lifts funding, but paying for it out of micro-grants thins the operational support a little. Acceptable — though raising the R$700K from sponsors would have protected both.`,
                    coach_r3_cut:           `<strong>You cut the one thing the funding KPI needs.</strong> Keeping the grant pool intact but axing demo days, investor matching and legal clinics leaves ventures well-run but unprepared for investors. Follow-on funding lands around R$16 milhões. Was R$700K the line to cut when the mandate measures funding raised?`,
                    coach_r3_thin:          `<strong>Spreading grants thinly yields weak signals.</strong> A little money everywhere closes no rounds. Concentrate the pool on the most fundable ventures so the push is large enough to matter.`,
                    coach_r3_starve:        `<strong>You starved your most fundable ventures.</strong> Too few mentor-hours on the two ventures closest to a round means those rounds don't close — the easiest follow-on funding you had, left on the table.`,
                    coach_r3_overconcentrate: `<strong>Over-concentration wastes hours.</strong> Pouring well over a thousand hours into two ventures hits diminishing returns and starves the rest of the cohort. A ~600–900 hour push closes the rounds; beyond that the marginal hour is wasted.`,
                    coach_r4_calibrated:    `<strong>Calibrated gates protect the metric honestly.</strong> Revenue traction, validated unit economics, a committed lead investor and mentor sign-off cull the zombies without gutting the cohort — the survival statistic now means something to the agency.`,
                    coach_r4_loose:         `<strong>Loose gates corrupt the very metric they're meant to satisfy.</strong> Graduate everyone still incorporated and the agency's two-year audit finds zombies — incorporated, no revenue, no team. Your 'survival rate' is inflated and your credibility, and your renewal, go with it.`,
                    coach_r4_brutal:        `<strong>Culling too hard collapses the cohort and the optics.</strong> Demanding a closed Series A from everyone protects the percentage but shrinks the cohort to an embarrassing size and strands ventures that would have made it. The gate is meant to calibrate, not to gut.`,
                    coach_r4_loyalty:       `<strong>Loyalty over reallocation just cost you a funding win.</strong> Pouring mentor-hours into two ventures that have missed every milestone, while a strong venture is starved of the support that would have closed its Series A, is sunk-cost thinking. When do you redirect support?`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>That is a board-ready answer.</strong> Calibrated gates, the discipline to off-ramp, and gated evidence — revenue, unit economics and committed investors per graduate — make your 60% real, not a paper number. That earns the refunding.`,
                    coach_r4_optics:        `<strong>Headcount optics is exactly the trap.</strong> 'We graduated everyone' is what inflates survival with zombies. The agency audits in two years; an optics answer with no gated evidence is how you lose the mandate even if the headline looked fine.`,
                    coach_r4_defer:         `<strong>'Trust the process' is not a defense.</strong> The board wants evidence now that your survival figure is real. Without leading evidence per graduate, deferring reads as having nothing to show — and the mandate is measured, not assumed.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Diagnosed the dual-KPI logic and the variance tension.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-diagnosed; leaned on a single venture type.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Deliberately diversified, variance managed.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Concentrated cohort — single-KPI or variance risk.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Concentrated support and invested in fundability.`,
                    sum_r3_equal:           `Equal allocation — taxed the winners.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Cut readiness or under-allocated to the fundable.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Calibrated gates, off-ramped, defended with evidence.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Loose/brutal gates or an optics defense.`,

                    insight_refunded:       `Refunded: you ran the incubator like a portfolio. A deliberately diversified cohort, support concentrated where it earned its keep, calibrated gates and the discipline to reallocate cleared both the 60% survival and R$25 milhões funding mandates with a real, defensible cohort.`,
                    insight_zombie:         `Met on paper: the headline numbers hit the mandate, but loose gates or an optics defense inflated survival with zombies. The agency's two-year audit will find non-operating ventures — the kind of 'success' that costs you the next renewal.`,
                    insight_survonly:       `Survival met, funding short: you protected the survival statistic — likely with safe SaaS — but follow-on funding fell short of R$25 milhões. The dual mandate forbids optimizing one metric; you needed higher-value ventures and a funded readiness program.`,
                    insight_fundonly:       `Funding met, survival short: you chased follow-on funding — likely with deep-tech swings — but the blended survival rate stayed below 60%. Exit value is not the survival the agency measures.`,
                    insight_lost:           `Mandate lost: you cleared neither KPI, so the agency cuts R$3,5 milhões, HTP halves staff and closes a cohort track. Revisit the portfolio mix, the allocation logic and the graduation gates — most likely you optimized a single metric or spread support too thinly.`,
                    insight_loweff:         `Resource efficiency stayed low: scarce mentor-hours and grants didn't reach the ventures that move the KPIs. Equal allocation, thin grants or loyalty to failing bets all bleed efficiency.`,
                    insight_zombies_flag:   `Zombie flag: your survival figure is partly non-operating ventures. Calibrated graduation gates and an evidence-based board defense are what make the metric real.`,
                    insight_disciplined:    `Portfolio discipline paid off: high resource efficiency means your scarce support concentrated on the ventures that actually moved survival and funding — the mark of a manager running a portfolio, not a charity.`,

                    hint_zombie:            `Your survival figure is inflating with zombies. Tighten the graduation gates and defend the cohort with gated evidence, or the agency's audit will undo it.`,
                    hint_eff:               `Resource efficiency is dangerously low — support isn't reaching the ventures that move the KPIs. Switch to tiered or milestone-gated allocation and stop subsidizing the doomed.`,
                    hint_behind:            `You're behind on both KPIs. Reconsider the cohort mix and concentrate scarce support on the most survivable and fundable ventures.`,
                    hint_funding:           `Survival is on track but follow-on funding is short. Fund the readiness program and concentrate grants and mentor-hours on the most fundable ventures.`,
                    hint_survival:          `Funding is on track but survival is below 60%. You're carrying too much variance — your cohort or gates need more reliable, survivable ventures.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — both KPIs on track and support reaching the right ventures. Lock it in with calibrated gates and an evidence-based board defense.`,

